## Advantage 3: Better RAM Usage

> "Microkernel operating systems tend to make better use of random access memory (RAM) than monolithic ones, since system processes that aren't implementing needed functionalities might be swapped out or destroyed."

### The Core Idea

In a **monolithic kernel**, the entire kernel is **always resident in RAM**:

```
Linux monolithic kernel RAM usage (typical server):
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Kernel text (code)          ~50–100 MB  (always in RAM)│
  │  Kernel data (structures)    ~20–50 MB   (always in RAM)│
  │  All loaded drivers          ~50–200 MB  (always in RAM)│
  │  All loaded filesystems      ~10–50 MB   (always in RAM)│
  │  Slab caches (kmalloc)       ~100–500 MB (always in RAM)│
  │  Page tables (all processes) ~50–200 MB  (always in RAM)│
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │
  │  TOTAL kernel RAM:         ~300–1100 MB (always resident)│
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Even if you're not using:
    • USB (no USB devices) → USB driver code still in RAM
    • Bluetooth (no BT) → Bluetooth stack still in RAM
    • NFS (no network mounts) → NFS code still in RAM
    • ext4 (only using XFS) → ext4 code still in RAM (if module loaded)
```

In a **microkernel**, only the microkernel is always resident:

```
seL4 microkernel RAM usage:
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Microkernel code              ~5–10 MB  (always in RAM)│
  │  Microkernel data              ~1–5 MB   (always in RAM)│
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │
  │  TOTAL always-resident:     ~10–15 MB                   │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  User-space servers (loaded on demand):
    • Filesystem server:      ~5–20 MB  (only if using files)
    • Network server:         ~10–50 MB (only if using network)
    • USB driver:             ~5–10 MB  (only if USB devices present)
    • GPU driver:             ~50–200 MB (only if using GPU)

  If you're not using USB:
    → USB driver process is NOT running
    → Its RAM is FREE (swapped out or destroyed)
    → Only the microkernel's 10–15 MB is resident
```

### The Numbers

| System | Always-Resident RAM | On-Demand RAM | Total (max) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Linux monolithic** (minimal config) | ~50 MB | ~500 MB (all modules) | ~550 MB |
| **Linux monolithic** (full desktop) | ~100 MB | ~1–2 GB (all drivers, FS, net) | ~1–2 GB |
| **seL4 microkernel** (bare) | **~10 MB** | 0 | **~10 MB** |
| **seL4 + basic services** | ~10 MB | ~50–100 MB | ~110 MB |
| **QNX** (automotive) | **~1 MB** (microkernel) | ~10–50 MB (drivers) | ~50 MB |

### Why This Matters

1. **Embedded systems**: A microcontroller with 64 KB of RAM can run a microkernel but not a monolithic kernel. QNX runs on systems with as little as **~1 MB RAM**.

2. **Security**: Smaller always-resident footprint = smaller attack surface. seL4's microkernel is **formally verified** — every line of the 8,700-line kernel has been mathematically proven correct. This is only feasible because the kernel is small.

3. **Predictability**: In real-time systems, you need to know the **worst-case memory usage**. A microkernel's 10 MB is fixed. A monolithic kernel's usage varies with loaded modules.

### The Tradeoff

"Swapped out or destroyed" sounds great, but it has costs:

```
If a server is swapped out and you need it:
  1. Page fault → kernel loads server from disk
  2. Server process starts up
  3. Server initializes (opens device, loads config)
  4. First request is processed

  This "cold start" can take milliseconds to seconds.
  In a monolithic kernel, the code is already in RAM — no cold start.

  This is why microkernels are better for:
    • Systems where you know which services you need (embedded, RTOS)
    • Systems where RAM is scarce
    • Systems where security is paramount (smaller attack surface)

  And why they're worse for:
    • General-purpose desktops (frequent cold starts)
    • Systems with many optional services (frequent swapping)
```

