## Summary: The Full Tradeoff

| Aspect | Monolithic | Microkernel |
|---|---|---|
| **Performance** | **Faster** (24 cycles for function call) | Slower (109–1,500 cycles for IPC) |
| **Overall overhead** | **1× (baseline)** | 1.03× (L4) to 2× (Mach) |
| **Modularity** | Low (tight coupling) | **High** (independent processes, clean interfaces) |
| **Portability** | Low (hardware code scattered) | **High** (hardware code in ~5K lines) |
| **RAM efficiency** | Low (all code always resident) | **High** (on-demand loading, ~10 MB base) |
| **Stability** | Low (one bug crashes everything) | **High** (bugs isolated to one server) |
| **Security** | Low (20M+ lines with full privileges) | **High** (~5K lines, formally verifiable) |
| **Complexity** | Simpler to develop (direct calls) | More complex (IPC design, interface management) |
| **Best for** | General-purpose, performance-critical | Embedded, RTOS, security-critical |

The passage's key point: **academic research favored microkernels** (because they're easier to verify, reason about, and prove correct) **but industry chose monolithic** (because performance won). The modern compromise is the **hybrid kernel** (Linux with loadable modules, macOS/XNU, Windows NT) — monolithic performance with some modularity.

